menu "SlateFPV configuration"

config CAMLINK_BENCH
    bool "Bench console (development only)"
    default n
    help
      DEVELOPMENT ONLY -- leave this off for anything that flies.

      Adds a line protocol on the USB serial port, driven by
      tools/slate-cli.py. Alongside read-only status it accepts commands
      that rebind the module to an arbitrary MAC, drop the camera link,
      clear BLE bonds and change record settings, none of which belong on
      a shipped unit.

        out:  #ST <link> <valid> <rec> <clip_s> <free_mb> <left_s> <batt>
              #FC <link> <armed> <boxarm_known> <bit> <nch> ch0..ch7 <rx> <crc>
        in:   'r' toggle record, '1' start, '0' stop

      Both halves run, so the flight controller can be brought up on its own
      with the camera switched off, and vice versa. Recording only ever changes
      on an explicit keypress.

config CAMLINK_SNIFF
    bool "DIAG: log every BLE advertisement seen while scanning"
    default n
    help
      Bring-up aid. In setup and bind modes, logs every BLE device heard --
      name, address, signal, connectable flag and the raw advert bytes -- so a
      camera the vendor filters do not recognise can be identified from what it
      actually broadcasts. Noisy; leave off for normal use.

config CAMLINK_GPSETTINGS_DUMP
    bool "DIAG: dump every GoPro setting the camera reports"
    default n
    help
      Registers for all GoPro settings and logs each id/value, to find out what
      a camera reports for resolution and framerate. Noisy; off for normal use.

config CAMLINK_USB_CONSOLE
    bool "USB console (command prompt on the USB-C port)"
    default y
    help
      A line-at-a-time prompt on the Supermini's native USB port, sharing it
      with the log: `st` for camera and flight-controller state, `rec` to
      start and stop recording, `scan` for the setup-mode camera list, and a
      few housekeeping commands. Type `help`.

      Reachable only with a cable in the port, which a flying module does not
      have, so it is on by default. It reads the receive FIFO directly and
      never installs the USB driver, so the log's behaviour with no host
      attached is unchanged.

choice CAMLINK_BLE_TX_POWER
    prompt "BLE transmit power"
    default CAMLINK_TX_N24
    help
      -24 dBm is correct in flight: the camera sits ~20 cm away, leaving about
      45 dB of margin, and staying quiet matters next to the ELRS receiver.

      It is a poor bench setting. Connection establishment needs the camera to
      hear us, and across a desk -24 dBm is marginal -- the symptom is a camera
      that is discovered reliably but whose connections time out.

    config CAMLINK_TX_N24
        bool "-24 dBm (flight default, camera ~20 cm)"
    config CAMLINK_TX_N12
        bool "-12 dBm (bench, camera on the same desk)"
    config CAMLINK_TX_N0
        bool "0 dBm (bench, camera across the room)"
    config CAMLINK_TX_P3
        bool "+3 dBm (maximum reach, not for flight)"
endchoice

config CAMLINK_BLE_BONDING
    bool "Bond / encrypt the BLE link to the camera"
    default y
    help
      Just Works bonding plus link encryption before speaking DUML.

      Required, not optional: unencrypted, the camera accepts the connection
      and then terminates it itself with reason 0x13 after about 16 s. Turn off
      only to reproduce the upstream DJI demo's behaviour.

config CAMLINK_BLE_CLEAR_BONDS
    bool "Clear stored BLE bonds at every boot"
    default n
    help
      Bring-up aid. A bond held on one side but forgotten by the other makes
      the next connection fail at link level with reason 0x3e.

      Leave OFF in normal use: once a camera is paired, deleting our half means
      the camera still expects an encrypted link we can no longer establish and
      every reconnect times out. Use the button's long-press re-bind instead,
      which clears both sides together.

config CAMLINK_BLE_SCAN_DEBUG
    bool "Log every BLE advertisement, GATT service and characteristic"
    default n
    help
      Dumps name, RSSI, manufacturer data and the DJI-filter verdict for every
      advertisement, plus the full GATT database and a one-shot read of every
      readable characteristic on connect.

      Diagnostic only -- it adds around a second to every connection. Use it
      when a camera is powered on but never connects: it distinguishes "not
      advertising" from "advertising something we reject", which otherwise look
      identical on the console.

choice CAMLINK_RECORD_MODE
    prompt "Record trigger mode"
    default CAMLINK_MODE_CUT
    config CAMLINK_MODE_ARM
        bool "ARM - arm starts recording, disarm stops"
    config CAMLINK_MODE_SWITCH
        bool "SWITCH - an RC channel controls recording"
    config CAMLINK_MODE_CUT
        bool "CUT - arm records; a press cuts and restarts the clip"

    config CAMLINK_MODE_BOTH
        bool "BOTH - arm records; a press toggles recording independently"
endchoice

config CAMLINK_SWITCH_CHANNEL
    int "RC switch channel (0-based; 6 = AUX3)"
    range 0 17
    default 6
    help
      First-boot default only -- the settings page writes the real value to
      NVS. MSP_RC returns rcData[] with the four sticks first, so AUX n is
      index n+3.

config CAMLINK_SWITCH_THRESHOLD
    int "RC switch window, lower edge (us)"
    range 900 2100
    default 1750
    help
      First-boot default only. The range is 900-2100 because that is what a
      receiver actually produces: CRSF and SBUS both put a switch at +100% on
      about 2012 us, so a limit of 2000 could not contain the very position the
      user was pointing at. See the scale note in camlink_cfg.h.

config CAMLINK_MSP_TX_GPIO
    int "UART1 TX GPIO (to FC RX)"
    default 4
    help
      Avoid GPIO2 and GPIO3. GPIO8 and GPIO9 carry the onboard LED and button
      and must stay free. Leave GPIO20/21 for the console.

config CAMLINK_MSP_RX_GPIO
    int "UART1 RX GPIO (from FC TX)"
    default 5

config CAMLINK_MSP_BAUD
    int "MSP baud rate"
    default 115200

endmenu
